 I researched the cultural and workplace differences between Slovenia, Greece and Belgium, the three countries where my selected Erasmus+ internships are located. 
The main areas considered are communication, workplace hierarchy, punctuality, working practices, language and cultural adaptation.

-----Slovenia – PRIOT Digital Systems, Maribor
Slovenian workplaces generally value professionalism, preparation, politeness and respect for experience. Business meetings are taken seriously, and employees are expected to arrive prepared and understand the purpose of the meeting. Slovenian communication can be relatively indirect, particularly with people who are not well known, and modesty is valued rather than openly boasting about achievements.

The workplace also has a structured approach to working hours. Full-time employment is generally 40 hours per week, normally spread over five days, with legal requirements concerning rest periods and overtime.

Potential differences for me:

I should prepare carefully before meetings.
I should communicate politely and avoid being overly direct.
I should respect managers and experienced colleagues.
I should not assume that a less direct answer means agreement.
I should be punctual and professional.
English should be useful in an international technology company, but learning some basic Slovenian would help with everyday life.

----Greece – JP IT Solutions & Consulting, Kavala
Greek workplace culture can place considerable importance on personal relationships and social interaction. 
Business meetings may begin with informal conversation before moving onto the main business discussion.
Meetings can also be more flexible than I might expect in Ireland, with agendas sometimes being treated as guidelines rather than followed rigidly.
This could be different from a more structured meeting environment.
I would therefore need to remain professional while also being comfortable with some informal conversation and relationship-building.

Potential differences for me:

Building a relationship with colleagues may be important.
Meetings may be more conversational.
Communication can be more expressive and animated.
I should still arrive on time even if local expectations around punctuality can be more flexible.
Professional appearance and good first impressions are important.
I would need to respect Greek workplace rules and company policies.

Adaptation strategy: I would be friendly and open to social interaction while maintaining professionalism.
I would also avoid assuming that every workplace follows exactly the same communication style as Ireland
-----Belgium – BMI Leisure, Geel
Belgian workplaces are likely to place strong importance on professional responsibility, agreed working arrangements and cooperation. Belgian employment guidance states that employees are expected to carry out their work carefully, honestly and accurately, follow reasonable employer instructions and maintain confidentiality concerning business and personal information.

This is particularly relevant to the BMI Leisure internship because the work involves software and hardware integration. I would need to communicate clearly with colleagues, follow technical procedures and take responsibility for testing and troubleshooting.

Potential differences for me:

I would need to follow agreed procedures carefully.
Confidential company information must be protected.
Team communication would be important when working with software and hardware.
I should be professional and reliable.
Belgium is multilingual, so I should not assume that everyone will communicate in the same language.
